    <string name="update_state_moving">Moving</string>
    <string name="update_state_still">Not moving</string>
    <string name="update_state_home_wifi">On one of their home wifi networks</string>
    <string name="update_state_live">Live tracking</string>
    <string name="update_state_on_demand">Only answers when asked</string>
    <string name="update_state_unknown">Unknown</string>
    <string name="update_body_moving">While they are moving, their phone sends its position at the rate they chose. It can arrive up to a minute later: the system groups several positions and delivers them at once, so the radio does not wake up every few seconds.</string>
    <string name="update_body_still">Their phone has noticed they have not moved for a while (from the physical activity sensor, or because their position is not changing) and has spaced updates out to one every 15 minutes, whatever rate they chose. On top of that, while stationary it only sends a position after moving more than 100 metres, so the map does not draw walks that never happened. It still sends a sign of life every quarter of an hour, repeating the position it already had, so that sitting still looks different from running out of battery. As soon as they move again it goes back to their rate straight away.</string>
    <string name="update_body_home_wifi">They are connected to a wifi network they marked as a fixed place (home, work, family homes). While they stay there, their phone updates once every 15 minutes instead of at their chosen rate: if they are not moving, asking every minute only drains the battery. Leaving that network restores their rate.</string>
    <string name="update_body_live">Someone is tracking them live: their phone sends its position every few seconds while it lasts, overriding whatever rate they chose.</string>
    <string name="update_body_on_demand">Their phone does not send its position on its own. It only answers when someone taps the refresh button on their card, so the time you see is the last time it was asked.</string>
    <string name="update_body_unknown">Their app is older than this version and does not send this yet, so there is no way to tell why it is taking so long.</string>
    <string name="update_info_overdue">It has been far longer than expected without any signal. The most likely causes are no internet (no data, no coverage or out of wifi range: a failed send is not retried, it waits for the next one) or a flat battery. It can also be power saving with a low battery, the phone being switched off, or the system having stopped their app.</string>
    <string name="update_info_footer">The time shown is when the last position reached the server, not the last time their phone checked where it was.</string>
